<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yolande, Duchess of Lorraine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1428">1428</a>–<a href="/years/1483">1483</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Yolande of Anjou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yolande of Anjou was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a> who inherited the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> in 1473. Her marriage to Ferry II of Vaudémont, son of René's old rival Antoine, reconciled the two branches of the house of Lorraine, and their son René II united Vaudémont with the duchy. She left the government of Lorraine to her son.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yolande-duchess-of-lorraine.jpg" alt="Yolande, Duchess of Lorraine" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Boudan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dessin_du_vitrail_de_Yolande_d%27Anjou_aux_Cordeliers_d%27Angers_(d%C3%A9tail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yolande was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> on 2 November 1428, the daughter of Isabella, Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a>, who was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, Duke of Anjou, Bar and Lorraine and Count of Provence. Her younger sister was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-anjou">Margaret of Anjou</a>, Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In 1445, at Nancy, she married her second cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick II</a>, Count of Vaudémont. The match was a dynastic alliance, arranged to end the dispute between her father and Frederick's father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoine-of-vaudemont">Antoine of Vaudémont</a>, over the succession to Lorraine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The couple had several children. Besides René, who became Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, there were Nicolas, Lord of Joinville and Bauffremont, who died in 1476; Jeanne, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv-duke-of-anjou">Charles IV, Duke of Anjou</a>, in 1474; Yolande, who married William II, Landgrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, in 1497; and Marguerite, who married René, Duke of Alençon, in 1488. Frederick died in 1470.</p><p>On the death of her nephew Nicolas in 1473 she inherited the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and passed it at once to her eldest son René II. In 1480, after the death of her father, she did the same with the Duchy of Bar. Though she nominally held major territories, she ceded power and titles to her husband and son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Yolande died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> on 23 March 1483, aged 54, on the birthday of her sister Margaret, who had died the previous summer. In 1845 the Danish poet Henrik Hertz made her the heroine of the verse drama King René's Daughter, in which she is a blind sixteen-year-old princess kept in a garden paradise until a Moorish physician restores her sight and Count Vaudémont falls in love with her. There is no evidence that she was ever blind.</p><p>The play was translated into many languages, and a Russian version, adapted by Vladimir Zotov, became the basis of Tchaikovsky's opera Iolanta, with a libretto by his brother Modest Ilyich Tchaikovsky, first performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a> on 18 December 1892. A silent film was made of it in 1913. The book The Holy Blood and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a> later named her a Grand Master of the Priory of Sion, a claim resting on documents forged in 1967.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yolande, Duchess of Lorraine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Antoine's son Ferry II married René's daughter <b>Yolande</b>, and their son René II inherited the Duchy of Lorraine in 1473, merging Vaudémont with it.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-vaudemont">County of Vaudémont</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nancy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Nancy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.69,6.18&amp;year=1463" aria-label="Nancy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4693 463 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yolande, Duchess of Lorraine"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5143 733h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5143 733h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.69,6.18&amp;year=1463">What was here in 1463? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
